      },
      "source": [
        "## Task 1: Dataset Identification and Justification"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3aaf7cff"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Dataset Selection: IMDB Movie Review Sentiment Classification\n",
        "\n",
        "**Dataset Name:** IMDB Movie Review Sentiment Classification\n",
        "\n",
        "**Publicly Available Link:** This dataset is widely available and can be directly loaded using TensorFlow/Keras datasets. It originates from the Large Movie Review Dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Type of Problem:** Natural Language Processing (NLP), Binary Text Classification.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Number of Classes and Samples:**\n",
        "*   **Classes:** 2 (Positive, Negative)\n",
        "*   **Samples:** 50,000 total reviews (25,000 for training, 25,000 for testing), with an equal number of positive and negative reviews in both sets.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Justification for CNN/LSTM/BiLSTM:**\n",
        "This dataset is highly suitable for various deep learning models:\n",
        "*   **LSTM/BiLSTM:** These models excel at processing sequential data like text. They can capture long-range dependencies and context within sentences, which is crucial for understanding sentiment.\n",
        "*   **CNN (1D CNN):** While traditionally used for images, 1D Convolutional Neural Networks are very effective for text classification. They can identify local patterns (n-grams) in the word sequences, which are indicative of sentiment, and are often faster to train than LSTMs.\n",
        "\n",
        "This dataset allows for a direct comparison of how these different architectures handle sequential text data for classification."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0c8199b0"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Task 2: Data Understanding and Preprocessing"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0f951f91"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Data Preprocessing: Padding Sequences and Train-Validation Split\n",
        "\n",
        "Since the movie reviews have varying lengths, we need to pad them to a fixed length. This ensures that all input sequences to our neural networks have a consistent shape. Reviews shorter than the `MAX_LEN` will be padded with zeros, and reviews longer than `MAX_LEN` will be truncated. We will use `tf.keras.preprocessing.sequence.pad_sequences` for this.\n",
        "\n",
        "Additionally, to properly evaluate model performance during training and prevent overfitting, we will split the original training data into a training set and a validation set."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "fbd750d3",
        "outputId": "ff3e2c87-fb24-4a5a-ec5f-3f2c678a8b67"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Shape of padded training data: (25000, 256)\n",
            "Shape of padded test data: (25000, 256)\n",
            "Shape of new training data (x_train): (15000, 256)\n",
            "Shape of new training labels (y_train): (15000,)\n",
            "Shape of validation data (x_val): (10000, 256)\n",
            "Shape of validation labels (y_val): (10000,)\n",
            "Shape of test data (test_data_padded): (25000, 256)\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "# Define maximum sequence length\n",
        "# We'll choose a length that covers most reviews without too much truncation or padding.\n",
        "# From previous output, max length is 2494, min is 11. Let's pick something reasonable like 256 or 500.\n",
        "# Let's start with 256 as a common choice for text classification.\n",
        "MAX_LEN = 256\n",
        "\n",
        "# Pad sequences to the chosen maximum length\n",
        "# 'post' padding means adding zeros at the end of the sequence\n",
        "# 'post' truncating means removing words from the end of the sequence if it's too long\n",
        "train_data_padded = tf.keras.preprocessing.sequence.pad_sequences(train_data,\n",
        "                                                        value=word_index[\"<pad>\"],\n",
        "                                                        padding='post',\n",
        "                                                        maxlen=MAX_LEN)\n",
        "\n",
        "test_data_padded = tf.keras.preprocessing.sequence.pad_sequences(test_data,\n",
        "                                                       value=word_index[\"<pad>\"],\n",
        "                                                       padding='post',\n",
        "                                                       maxlen=MAX_LEN)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Shape of padded training data: {train_data_padded.shape}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Shape of padded test data: {test_data_padded.shape}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Split training data into training and validation sets\n",
        "# We'll use 10,000 samples for validation\n",
        "NUM_VALIDATION_SAMPLES = 10000\n",
        "\n",
        "x_val = train_data_padded[:NUM_VALIDATION_SAMPLES]\n",
        "y_val = train_labels[:NUM_VALIDATION_SAMPLES]\n",
        "\n",
        "x_train = train_data_padded[NUM_VALIDATION_SAMPLES:]\n",
        "y_train = train_labels[NUM_VALIDATION_SAMPLES:]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Shape of new training data (x_train): {x_train.shape}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Shape of new training labels (y_train): {y_train.shape}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Shape of validation data (x_val): {x_val.shape}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Shape of validation labels (y_val): {y_val.shape}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Shape of test data (test_data_padded): {test_data_padded.shape}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
